NR1, signed tonight as the direction (his words in the brief above; it builds on CH1): the book is told by its cast. He is a persona too, his voice the default narrator; the agents come in turn and tell the story from their side: what each saw, the problem it hit, what worked and why (the prompts; the anchored data that keeps it from hallucinating; the context that grows until things are lost). Very visual: each agent's mood on the page (happy, worried, stressed, unhappy). Interviews between agents, a therapy session, an agent complaining. Each agent a distinct persona (clothes, hair, almost a culture, a little stereotypical, like the icons), introduced with a scorecard like a Top Trumps card; the reader always knows which agents exist at that moment. Narrative first: hashes and ids leave the page for the online reader's augmented mode. First on the opening pages (the ten-page sample and a couple more). Guard rails: scenes are dramatised, marked as such, and rest on the record; what an agent says about how it works must be true; the failures stay in; nothing attack-shaped; no real person but him, at his request.
Yours: for each agent, the record each scene can rest on (did, got wrong, said, with commits), and the cast at every moment of the day: which agents existed when, so the book can show who is on stage on each page.

